@node	2.3, 2.4, 2.2, Chapter 2
@section Symbolic Data

All the compound data objects we have used so far were constructed ultimately
from numbers.  In this section we extend the representational capability of our
language by introducing the ability to work with arbitrary symbols as data.

@menu
* 2.3.1::            Quotation
* 2.3.2::            Example: Symbolic Differentiation
* 2.3.3::            Example: Representing Sets
* 2.3.4::            Example: Huffman Encoding Trees
@end menu

@node	2.3.1, 2.3.2, 2.3, 2.3
@subsection Quotation

If we can form compound data using symbols, we can have lists such as

@example kotlin
vlist(VSym("a"), VSym("b"), VSym("c"), VSym("d"))
vlist(VInt(23), VInt(45), VInt(17))
vlist(
    vlist(VSym("Norah"), VInt(12)),
    vlist(VSym("Molly"), VInt(9)),
    vlist(VSym("Anna"), VInt(7)),
    vlist(VSym("Lauren"), VInt(6)),
    vlist(VSym("Charlotte"), VInt(4)),
)
@end example

@noindent
Lists containing symbols can look just like the expressions of our
language:

@example kotlin
vlist(VSym("*"),
        vlist(VSym("+"),
        VInt(23),
    VInt(45)),
        vlist(VSym("+"),
    VSym("x"), VInt(9)))
@end example

@noindent
In order to manipulate symbols we need a new element in our language: the
ability to @newterm{quote} a data object.  In the original book's language this ability matters
because a sequence of names in parentheses is otherwise ambiguous: it
could be a call to a function named @code{a}, or a list of three symbols.  Kotlin's syntax never
carries that ambiguity.  A call always looks like @code{a(b, c)}; a @code{Value}
literal always looks like @code{vlist(VSym("a"), VSym("b"), VSym("c"))}.
There is no reading of the second form under which it means anything other
than a three-element list, so this edition needs nothing to quote:
@code{vlist(VSym("a"), VSym("b"))} already, directly, is a list of two symbols,
with no separate mark disambiguating it from a function call.@footnote{A Kotlin string literal such as @code{"a"} is a @code{String}.  @code{VSym("a")} wraps that name as a symbol value.  Symbol equality compares the stored names by content; the constructor does not intern them.  Kotlin's double quotes always introduce a string literal.}

This confusion is well known in the context of natural languages, where
words and sentences may be regarded either as semantic entities or as
character strings (syntactic entities).  The common practice in natural
languages is to use quotation marks to indicate that a word or a sentence is
to be treated literally as a string of characters.  For instance, the first
letter of ``John'' is clearly ``J.''  If we tell somebody ``say your name
aloud,'' we expect to hear that person's name.  However, if we tell somebody
``say `your name' aloud,'' we expect to hear the words ``your name.''  Note
that we are forced to nest quotation marks to describe what somebody else
might say.@footnote{Allowing quotation in a language wreaks havoc with the
ability to reason about the language in simple terms, because it destroys the
notion that equals can be substituted for equals.  For example, three is one
plus two, but the word ``three'' is not the phrase ``one plus two.''
Quotation is powerful because it gives us a way to build expressions that
manipulate other expressions (as we will see when we write an interpreter in
@ref{Chapter 4}). But allowing statements in a language that talk about other
statements in that language makes it very difficult to maintain any coherent
principle of what ``equals can be substituted for equals'' should mean.  For
example, if we know that the evening star is the morning star, then from the
statement ``the evening star is Venus'' we can deduce ``the morning star is
Venus.''  However, given that ``John knows that the evening star is Venus''
we cannot infer that ``John knows that the morning star is Venus.''}

We can follow this same practice to identify lists and symbols that are to be
treated as data objects rather than as expressions to be evaluated.  Now we
can distinguish between symbols and their values:

@example kotlin
val a = VInt(1)
val b = VInt(2)

vlist(a, b)
// => (1 2)

vlist(VSym("a"), VSym("b"))
// => (a b)

vlist(VSym("a"), b)
// => (a 2)
@end example

@noindent
The first call builds a list of the @newterm{values} bound to @code{a} and
@code{b}; the second builds a list of the two symbols themselves, which share
nothing with the variables above but their spelling; the third mixes a symbol
with a bound value, exactly as freely as the first two, because @code{Value}
is one dynamic union covering every kind of datum this language has.

@noindent
Quotation also allows the book to type in compound objects using the
conventional printed representation for lists.@footnote{Strictly, the book's
quotation mark violates the general rule that all compound expressions in
the original book's language are delimited by parentheses and look like
lists; a leading quote mark is a single-character abbreviation that a
reader expands into a two-element list of the symbol @code{quote} and the
expression before the interpreter ever sees it.  This
edition has no reader to do that expanding, so there was never a second,
abbreviated spelling to reconcile with a first: @code{vlist(VSym("car"),
vlist(VSym("quote"), vlist(VSym("a"), VSym("b"), VSym("c"))))} already is the
direct, and only, way to build that nested list.  Exercise 2.55 works out
what a reader's expansion rule would have built, by hand, for doubled
quotation.}

@example kotlin
val abc = vlist(VSym("a"), VSym("b"), VSym("c"))
(abc as VPair).car
// => a

abc.cdr
// => (b c)
@end example

@noindent
In keeping with this, the empty list is simply @code{VNil}, the runtime's own
singleton, so there is no separate variable to dispense with the way the
book's discussion of @code{nil} implies.

One additional primitive used in manipulating symbols is @code{==} on
@code{VSym}, which takes two symbols and tests whether they are the
same.@footnote{Because a boxed @code{VSym} value's @code{equals} already
compares its wrapped name, @code{==} is exactly the book's ``consist of the
same characters in the same order'' definition of symbol sameness.  Such a
definition skirts a deep issue that we are not yet ready to address: the
meaning of ``sameness'' in a programming language more generally, for values
that are not symbols.  We will return to this in @ref{Chapter 3}
(@ref{3.1.3}).}  Using @code{==}, we can implement a useful procedure called
@code{memq}.  This takes two arguments, an item and a @code{Value} chain.  If the
item is not contained in the chain (i.e., is not @code{==} to any item in
the chain), then @code{memq} returns @code{VBool(false)}.  Otherwise, it
returns the sublist beginning with the first occurrence of the item:

@example kotlin
fun memq(item: Value, x: Value): Value =
    when @{
        x is VPair && x.car == item -> x
        x is VPair -> memq(item, x.cdr)
        else -> VBool(false)
    @}
@end example

@noindent
For example, the value of

@example kotlin
memq(VSym("apple"),
        vlist(VSym("pear"),
        VSym("banana"),
    VSym("prune")))
@end example

@noindent
is @code{#f}, whereas the value of

@example kotlin
memq(
    VSym("apple"),
    vlist(VSym("x"),
            vlist(VSym("apple"),
        VSym("sauce")),
        VSym("y"),
        VSym("apple"), VSym("pear")),
)
@end example

@noindent
is @code{(apple pear)}: the first @code{apple} is buried inside a nested
list, a different @code{Value} from the bare symbol @code{VSym("apple")} being
searched for, so it is skipped exactly as the book's untagged sublist is
skipped by its pointer-equality test.

@quotation
@emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 2.53 with a host-language exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.53}Exercise 2.53:} What value, or what
printed form, does each of the following produce?  Answer from the types
alone where you can, without running anything.

@example kotlin
vlist(VSym("a"), VSym("b"), VSym("c"))
vlist(vlist(VSym("george")))

val pairs = vlist(
    vlist(VSym("x1"), VSym("x2")),
    vlist(VSym("y1"), VSym("y2")),
)
pairs.cdr
(pairs.cdr as VPair).car

val shortList = vlist(VSym("a"),
    VSym("short"),
    VSym("list"))
(shortList as VPair).car is VPair

memq(
    VSym("red"),
    vlist(
        vlist(VSym("red"), VSym("shoes")),
        vlist(VSym("blue"), VSym("socks")),
    ),
)
memq(
    VSym("red"),
    vlist(
        VSym("red"),
        VSym("shoes"),
        VSym("blue"),
        VSym("socks"),
    ),
)
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.54}Exercise 2.54:} Two @code{Value} lists are said to
be @code{myEqual} if they contain equal elements arranged in the same order.
For example,

@example kotlin
myEqual(
    vlist(VSym("this"),
        VSym("is"),
        VSym("a"),
        VSym("list")),
    vlist(VSym("this"),
        VSym("is"),
        VSym("a"),
        VSym("list")),
)
@end example

@noindent
is true, but

@example kotlin
myEqual(
    vlist(VSym("this"),
        VSym("is"),
        VSym("a"),
        VSym("list")),
    vlist(
        VSym("this"),
        vlist(VSym("is"), VSym("a")),
        VSym("list"),
    ),
)
@end example

@noindent
is false.  To be more precise, we can define @code{myEqual} recursively in
terms of the basic @code{==} equality of symbols by saying that @code{a} and
@code{b} are @code{myEqual} if they are both symbols and the symbols are
@code{==}, or if they are both lists such that @code{a}'s car is
@code{myEqual} to @code{b}'s car and @code{a}'s cdr is @code{myEqual} to
@code{b}'s cdr.  Using this idea, implement @code{myEqual} as a
function.@footnote{In practice, programmers use @code{myEqual} to compare
lists that contain numbers as well as symbols.  Numbers are not considered to
be symbols.  The question of whether two numerically equal numbers (as
tested by @code{==} on @code{VInt}) are also identical is highly
implementation-dependent.  A more thorough definition of @code{myEqual} would
also stipulate that if @code{a} and @code{b} are both numbers, then @code{a}
and @code{b} are @code{myEqual} if they are numerically equal.}
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 2.55 with a host-language exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.55}Exercise 2.55:} SICP's Eva
Lu Ator types to her interpreter a doubly quoted symbol,
and to her surprise the interpreter prints back the symbol @code{quote}.  Kotlin has no
reader, so a quoted spelling is never something a running program types at
a prompt, but the reader's expansion rule is simple enough to apply by hand:
a single quote before a datum is sugar for the two-element list holding the
symbol @code{quote} and the datum, so a doubled quote is sugar for a
quotation of a quotation.  The edition models that nesting directly: each
@code{QuotationForm.Quoted} node is one quote layer over its operand, and
@code{QuotationForm.Name} is the symbol.  Build the tree for the doubly
quoted symbol @code{abracadabra} and return the outer node's operand.
Explain, in terms of the tree's own shape rather than by appealing to a
reader, why that operand is a quotation node rather than the name
@code{abracadabra}.
@end quotation

@node	2.3.2, 2.3.3, 2.3.1, 2.3
@subsection Example: Symbolic Differentiation

As an illustration of symbol manipulation and a further illustration of data
abstraction, consider the design of a procedure that performs symbolic
differentiation of algebraic expressions.  We would like the procedure to take
as arguments an algebraic expression and a variable and to return the
derivative of the expression with respect to the variable.  For example, if the
arguments to the procedure are @math{{ax^2 + bx + c}} and @math{x}, the
procedure should return @math{{2ax + b}}.  Symbolic differentiation is of
special historical significance in Lisp.  It was one of the motivating examples
behind the development of a computer language for symbol manipulation.
Furthermore, it marked the beginning of the line of research that led to the
development of powerful systems for symbolic mathematical work, which are
currently being used by a growing number of applied mathematicians and
physicists.

In developing the symbolic-differentiation program, we will follow the same
strategy of data abstraction that we followed in developing the rational-number
system of @ref{2.1.1}.  That is, we will first define a differentiation
algorithm that operates on abstract objects such as ``sums,'' ``products,'' and
``variables'' without worrying about how these are to be represented.  Only
afterward will we address the representation problem.

@subsubheading The differentiation program with abstract data

In order to keep things simple, we will consider a very simple
symbolic-differentiation program that handles expressions that are built up
using only the operations of addition and multiplication with two arguments.
Differentiation of any such expression can be carried out by applying the
following reduction rules:
@displaymath
{{dc \over dx} \,=\, 0,} 
  \quad {\hbox{for } c \hbox{ a constant }} {\hbox{or a variable }} {\hbox{different from } x,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
dc
-- = 0  for c a constant, or a variable different from x
dx

dx
-- = 1
dx

d(u + v)   du   dv
-------- = -- + --
   dx      dx   dx

d(uv)     / dv \     / du \
----- = u | -- | + v | -- |
 dx       \ dx /     \ dx /
@end example

@end ifinfo
@displaymath
\eqalign{
{dx \over dx}         &=   1, \cr
{d(u + v) \over dx}   &=   {du \over dx} + {dv \over dx}, \cr
{d(uv) \over dx}      &=   u \kern0.1em {dv \over dx} + v \kern0.1em {du \over dx}.
}
@end displaymath
@noindent
Observe that the latter two rules are recursive in nature.  That is, to obtain
the derivative of a sum we first find the derivatives of the terms and add
them.  Each of the terms may in turn be an expression that needs to be
decomposed.  Decomposing into smaller and smaller pieces will eventually
produce pieces that are either constants or variables, whose derivatives will
be either 0 or 1.

To embody these rules we indulge in a little wishful thinking, as we did in
designing the rational-number implementation.  If we had a means for
representing algebraic expressions, we should be able to tell whether an
expression is a sum, a product, a constant, or a variable.  We should be able
to extract the parts of an expression.  For a sum, for example, we want to be
able to extract the addend (first term) and the augend (second term).  Where
the book assumes ten free-floating procedures for this (@code{variable?},
@code{same-variable?}, @code{sum?}, @code{addend}, @code{augend},
@code{make-sum}, @code{product?}, @code{multiplier}, @code{multiplicand},
@code{make-product}), one sealed hierarchy grants all of them at once:

@example kotlin
sealed interface Expr @{
    data class Num(val n: Long) : Expr
    data class Var(val name: String) : Expr
    data class Sum(val a1: Expr, val a2: Expr) : Expr
    data class Product(val a1: Expr,
        val a2: Expr) : Expr
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{sum?} and @code{product?} collapse into the @code{is Expr.Sum}/@code{is
Expr.Product} branches below; @code{addend}/@code{augend} and
@code{multiplier}/@code{multiplicand} become the two fields @code{a1}/@code{a2}
those branches bind; @code{make-sum}/@code{make-product} survive as ordinary
functions, unchanged in name.  @code{variable?} is absorbed into the
@code{is Expr.Var} branch, and @code{same-variable?} is just @code{==} on
the two variables' @code{name} fields.  Using pattern matching in place of
the primitive predicate @code{number?}, we can express the differentiation
rules as the following function:

@example kotlin
fun deriv(exp: Expr, variable: String): Expr =
    when (exp) @{
        is Expr.Num -> Expr.Num(0)
        is Expr.Var -> Expr.Num(if (exp.name == variable) 1 else 0)
        is Expr.Sum -> Expr.Sum(deriv(exp.a1,
            variable),
                deriv(exp.a2,
            variable))
        is Expr.Product ->
            Expr.Sum(
                Expr.Product(exp.a1,
                        deriv(exp.a2,
                    variable)),
                Expr.Product(deriv(exp.a1,
                    variable),
                    exp.a2),
            )
    @}
@end example

@noindent
This @code{deriv} function incorporates the complete differentiation
algorithm.  Since @code{Expr}'s four variants are exhaustive, the compiler
proves every case is handled; the book's final error clause for an unknown expression type has no
counterpart here, because there
is no fifth variant left for it to catch.

@subsubheading Representing algebraic expressions

Printing an expression back in the book's parenthesized prefix notation is
a small recursive function over the same four variants:

@example kotlin
fun printExpr(e: Expr): String =
    when (e) @{
        is Expr.Num -> e.n.toString()
        is Expr.Var -> e.name
        is Expr.Sum -> "(+ $@{printExpr(e.a1)@} $@{printExpr(e.a2)@})"
        is Expr.Product ->
            "(* $@{printExpr(e.a1)@} $@{printExpr(e.a2)@})"
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Let us look at some examples of @code{deriv}'s behavior, built with the
naive constructors @code{Expr.Sum}/@code{Expr.Product} shown above:

@example kotlin
val sum = Expr.Sum(Expr.Var("x"), Expr.Num(3))
printExpr(deriv(sum, "x"))
// => (+ 1 0)

val product = Expr.Product(Expr.Var("x"), Expr.Var("y"))
printExpr(deriv(product, "x"))
// => (+ (* x 0) (* 1 y))

val nested = Expr.Product(Expr.Product(Expr.Var("x"),
    Expr.Var("y")),
    Expr.Sum(Expr.Var("x"), Expr.Num(3)))
printExpr(deriv(nested, "x"))
// => (+ (* (* x y) (+ 1 0)) (* (+ (* x 0) (* 1 y)) (+ x
// 3)))
@end example

@noindent
The program produces answers that are correct; however, they are unsimplified.
It is true that
@displaymath
{d(xy) \over dx} \,=\, {x \cdot 0} + {1 \cdot y,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
d(xy)
----- = x * 0 + 1 * y
 dx
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
but we would like the program to know that @math{{x \cdot 0 = 0}}, @math{{1 \cdot y = y}},
and @math{{0 + y = y}}.  The answer for the second example should have been
simply @code{y}.  As the third example shows, this becomes a serious issue when
the expressions are complex.

Our difficulty is much like the one we encountered with the rational-number
implementation: we haven't reduced answers to simplest form.  To accomplish the
rational-number reduction, we needed to change only the constructors and the
selectors of the implementation.  We can adopt a similar strategy here.  We
won't change @code{deriv} at all.  Instead, we will change @code{makeSum} so
that if both summands are numbers, @code{makeSum} will add them and return
their sum.  Also, if one of the summands is 0, then @code{makeSum} will return
the other summand:

@example kotlin
fun isNumber(e: Expr,
    n: Long): Boolean = e is Expr.Num &&
    e.n == n

fun makeSum(a1: Expr, a2: Expr): Expr =
    when @{
        isNumber(a1, 0) -> a2
        isNumber(a2, 0) -> a1
        a1 is Expr.Num &&
            a2 is Expr.Num -> Expr.Num(a1.n + a2.n)
        else -> Expr.Sum(a1, a2)
    @}
@end example

@noindent
This uses the function @code{isNumber}, which checks whether an expression is
equal to a given number.  Similarly, we will change @code{makeProduct} to
build in the rules that 0 times anything is 0 and 1 times anything is the
thing itself:

@example kotlin
fun makeProduct(m1: Expr, m2: Expr): Expr =
    when @{
        isNumber(m1,
            0) || isNumber(m2,
            0) -> Expr.Num(0)
        isNumber(m1, 1) -> m2
        isNumber(m2, 1) -> m1
        m1 is Expr.Num &&
            m2 is Expr.Num -> Expr.Num(m1.n * m2.n)
        else -> Expr.Product(m1, m2)
    @}

fun deriv(exp: Expr, variable: String): Expr =
    when (exp) @{
        is Expr.Num -> Expr.Num(0)
        is Expr.Var -> Expr.Num(if (exp.name == variable) 1 else 0)
        is Expr.Sum -> makeSum(deriv(exp.a1,
            variable),
                deriv(exp.a2,
            variable))
        is Expr.Product ->
            makeSum(
                makeProduct(exp.a1,
                        deriv(exp.a2,
                    variable)),
                makeProduct(deriv(exp.a1,
                    variable),
                    exp.a2),
            )
    @}
@end example

@noindent
This edition's numbers are checked @code{Long}s, so a differentiation that
overflows fails loudly rather than wrapping silently, exactly as
@ref{2.1.3}'s exact-integer convention promises everywhere else in this book.
Here is how this version works on our three examples:

@example kotlin
printExpr(deriv(sum, "x"))
// => 1

printExpr(deriv(product, "x"))
// => y

printExpr(deriv(nested, "x"))
// => (+ (* x y) (* y (+ x 3)))
@end example

@noindent
Although this is quite an improvement, the third example shows that there is
still a long way to go before we get a program that puts expressions into a
form that we might agree is ``simplest.''  The problem of algebraic
simplification is complex because, among other reasons, a form that may be
simplest for one purpose may not be for another.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.56}Exercise 2.56:} Show how to extend the basic
differentiator to handle more kinds of expressions.  For instance, implement
the differentiation rule
@displaymath
{d(u^{\kern0.1ex n}) \over dx} \,=\, {nu^{\kern0.1ex n-1} \, {du \over dx}}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
d(u^n)            du
------ = nu^(n-1) --  
  dx              dx
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
by adding a fifth variant to a local copy of @code{Expr} named @code{PowExpr}
(kept local to this exercise, per the section's one-representation-per-
exercise convention, rather than editing the running prose's @code{Expr}) and
a matching @code{Pow} branch to a local copy of @code{deriv}, plus a
@code{makeExponentiation} constructor.  Print exponentiation as @code{(** base
n)}.  Build in the rules that anything raised to the power 0 is 1 and
anything raised to the power 1 is the thing itself.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.57}Exercise 2.57:} Extend the differentiation
program to handle sums and products of arbitrary numbers of (two or more)
terms.  Then the last example above could be expressed as

@example kotlin
deriv(
    NaryExpr.Product(
        listOf(
            NaryExpr.Var("x"),
            NaryExpr.Var("y"),
            NaryExpr.Sum(
                listOf(
                    NaryExpr.Var("x"),
                    NaryExpr.Num(3),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
    "x",
)
@end example

Try to do this by changing only the representation for sums and products
(your own local @code{NaryExpr}, holding a @code{List} of terms or factors
instead of a fixed pair), without changing @code{deriv}'s own shape at all.
For example, the addend of a sum would be the first term, and the augend
would be the sum of the rest of the terms.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.58}Exercise 2.58:} Suppose we want to modify the
differentiation program so that it works with ordinary mathematical notation,
in which @code{+} and @code{*} are infix rather than prefix operators.  Since
the differentiation program is defined in terms of abstract data, we can
modify it to work with different concrete syntax solely by changing what
builds an @code{Expr} in the first place, leaving @code{deriv} itself
untouched.

@enumerate a

@item
Write a tokenizer and a recursive-descent parser from a @code{String} into
this section's @code{Expr}, to differentiate algebraic expressions presented
in infix form, such as @code{"(x + (3 * (x + (y + 2))))"}.  To simplify the
task, assume that @code{+} and @code{*} always take two arguments and that
expressions are fully parenthesized.

@item
The problem becomes substantially harder if we allow standard algebraic
notation, such as @code{"x + 3 * (x + y + 2)"}, which drops unnecessary
parentheses and assumes that multiplication is done before addition.  Extend
your parser to handle operator precedence so that our derivative program
still works.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@node	2.3.3, 2.3.4, 2.3.2, 2.3
@subsection Example: Representing Sets

In the previous examples we built representations for two kinds of compound
data objects: rational numbers and algebraic expressions.  In one of these
examples we had the choice of simplifying (reducing) the expressions at either
construction time or selection time, but other than that the choice of a
representation for these structures in terms of lists was straightforward. When
we turn to the representation of sets, the choice of a representation is not so
obvious.  Indeed, there are a number of possible representations, and they
differ significantly from one another in several ways.

Informally, a set is simply a collection of distinct objects.  To give a more
precise definition we can employ the method of data abstraction.  That is, we
define ``set'' by specifying the operations that are to be used on sets.  These
are @code{unionSet}, @code{intersectionSet}, @code{elementOfSet}, and
@code{adjoinSet}.  @code{elementOfSet} is a predicate that determines whether a
given element is a member of a set.  @code{adjoinSet} takes an object and a
set as arguments and returns a set that contains the elements of the original
set and also the adjoined element.  @code{unionSet} computes the union of two
sets, which is the set containing each element that appears in either
argument.  @code{intersectionSet} computes the intersection of two sets, which
is the set containing only elements that appear in both arguments.  From the
viewpoint of data abstraction, we are free to design any representation that
implements these operations in a way consistent with the interpretations
given above.  This edition fixes the element type to @code{Long} across all
three representations, unordered included, so that one consistent element
type carries all the way to the ordered and tree representations, which need
@code{<}/@code{>} anyway.@footnote{If we want to be more formal, we can
specify ``consistent with the interpretations given above'' to mean that the
operations satisfy a collection of rules such as these:

@itemize @bullet

@item
For any set @code{s} and any object @code{x},
@code{elementOfSet(x, adjoinSet(x, s))}
is true (informally: ``Adjoining an object to a
set produces a set that contains the object'').

@item
For any sets @code{s} and @code{t} and any object @code{x},
@code{elementOfSet(x, unionSet(s, t))}
is equal to
@code{elementOfSet(x, s) || elementOfSet(x, t)}
(informally: ``The elements of the union of @code{s} and @code{t} are the
elements that are in @code{s} or in @code{t}'').

@item
For any object @code{x},
@code{elementOfSet(x, emptyList())}
is false (informally: ``No object is an element of the empty set'').

@end itemize
}

@subsubheading Sets as unordered lists

One way to represent a set is as a @code{List} of its elements in which no
element appears more than once.  The empty set is represented by the empty
list.  In this representation, @code{elementOfSet} is similar to the
function @code{memq} of @ref{2.3.1}, except that it compares @code{Long}s
with @code{==} directly, since this edition's sets are never nested:

@example kotlin
fun elementOfSet(x: Long, set: List<Long>): Boolean =
    when @{
        set.isEmpty() -> false
        x == set[0] -> true
        else -> elementOfSet(x, set.drop(1))
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Using this, we can write @code{adjoinSet}.  If the object to be adjoined is
already in the set, we just return the set unchanged.  Otherwise, we build a
new list with the object in front:

@example kotlin
fun adjoinSet(x: Long, set: List<Long>): List<Long> =
    if (elementOfSet(x, set)) set else listOf(x) + set
@end example

@noindent
For @code{intersectionSet} we can use a recursive strategy.  If we know how to
form the intersection of @code{set2} and the tail of @code{set1}, we only
need to decide whether to include the head of @code{set1} in this.  But this
depends on whether that head is also in @code{set2}.  Here is the resulting
function:

@example kotlin
fun intersectionSet(set1: List<Long>,
    set2: List<Long>): List<Long> =
    when @{
        set1.isEmpty() || set2.isEmpty() -> emptyList()
        elementOfSet(set1[0], set2) -> listOf(set1[0]) +
            intersectionSet(set1.drop(1), set2)
        else -> intersectionSet(set1.drop(1), set2)
    @}
@end example

@noindent
In designing a representation, one of the issues we should be concerned with is
efficiency.  Consider the number of steps required by our set operations.
Since they all use @code{elementOfSet}, the speed of this operation has a
major impact on the efficiency of the set implementation as a whole.  Now, in
order to check whether an object is a member of a set, @code{elementOfSet}
may have to scan the entire set. (In the worst case, the object turns out not
to be in the set.)  Hence, if the set has @math{n} elements,
@code{elementOfSet}  might take up to @math{n} steps.  Thus, the number of
steps required grows as @math{{\Theta(n)}}.  The number of steps required by
@code{adjoinSet}, which uses this operation, also grows as @math{{\Theta(n)}}.
For @code{intersectionSet}, which does an @code{elementOfSet} check for
each element of @code{set1}, the number of steps required grows as the product
of the sizes of the sets involved, or @math{{\Theta(n^2)}} for two sets of size
@math{n}.  The same will be true of @code{unionSet}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.59}Exercise 2.59:} Implement the @code{unionSet}
operation for the unordered-list representation of sets.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.60}Exercise 2.60:} We specified that a set would be
represented as a list with no duplicates.  Now suppose we allow duplicates.
For instance, the set @math{{\{1, 2, 3\}}} could be represented as the list @code{[2, 3, 2, 1,
3, 2, 2]}.  Design functions @code{elementOfSetDup}, @code{adjoinSetDup},
@code{unionSetDup}, and @code{intersectionSetDup} that operate on this
representation.  How does the efficiency of each compare with the corresponding
function for the non-duplicate representation?  Are there applications for
which you would use this representation in preference to the non-duplicate one?
@end quotation

@subsubheading Sets as ordered lists

One way to speed up our set operations is to change the representation so that
the set elements are listed in increasing order.  Because this edition's sets
are already the numeric type @code{Long}, we can compare elements directly
with @code{<} and @code{>} without needing to agree on a way to order
non-numeric objects first.  Whereas our first representation above allowed us
to represent the set @math{{\{1, 3, 6, 10\}}} by listing the elements in any
order, our new representation allows only the list @code{[1, 3, 6, 10]}.

One advantage of ordering shows up in @code{elementOfSetOrdered}: In checking
for the presence of an item, we no longer have to scan the entire set.  If we
reach a set element that is larger than the item we are looking for, then we
know that the item is not in the set:

@example kotlin
fun elementOfSetOrdered(x: Long,
    set: List<Long>): Boolean =
    when @{
        set.isEmpty() -> false
        x == set[0] -> true
        x < set[0] -> false
        else -> elementOfSetOrdered(x, set.drop(1))
    @}
@end example

How many steps does this save?  In the worst case, the item we are looking for
may be the largest one in the set, so the number of steps is the same as for
the unordered representation.  On the other hand, if we search for items of
many different sizes we can expect that sometimes we will be able to stop
searching at a point near the beginning of the list and that other times we
will still need to examine most of the list.  On the average we should expect
to have to examine about half of the items in the set.  Thus, the average
number of steps required will be about @math{{n / 2}}.  This is still
@math{{\Theta(n)}} growth, but it does save us, on the average, a factor of 2
in number of steps over the previous implementation.

We obtain a more impressive speedup with @code{intersectionSetOrdered}.  In
the unordered representation this operation required @math{{\Theta(n^2)}}
steps, because we performed a complete scan of @code{set2} for each element of
@code{set1}.  But with the ordered representation, we can use a more clever
method.  Begin by comparing the initial elements, @code{x1} and @code{x2}, of
the two sets.  If @code{x1} equals @code{x2}, then that gives an element of the
intersection, and the rest of the intersection is the intersection of the
tails of the two sets.  Suppose, however, that @code{x1} is less than
@code{x2}.  Since @code{x2} is the smallest element in @code{set2}, we can
immediately conclude that @code{x1} cannot appear anywhere in @code{set2} and
hence is not in the intersection.  Hence, the intersection is equal to the
intersection of @code{set2} with the tail of @code{set1}.  Similarly, if
@code{x2} is less than @code{x1}, then the intersection is given by the
intersection of @code{set1} with the tail of @code{set2}.  Here is the
function:

@example kotlin
fun intersectionSetOrdered(set1: List<Long>,
    set2: List<Long>): List<Long> @{
    if (set1.isEmpty() || set2.isEmpty()) return emptyList()
    val x1 = set1[0]
    val x2 = set2[0]
    return when @{
        x1 == x2 -> listOf(x1) + intersectionSetOrdered(set1.drop(1),
            set2.drop(1))
        x1 < x2 -> intersectionSetOrdered(set1.drop(1),
            set2)
        else -> intersectionSetOrdered(set1,
            set2.drop(1))
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To estimate the number of steps required by this process, observe that at each
step we reduce the intersection problem to computing intersections of smaller
sets---removing the first element from @code{set1} or @code{set2} or both.
Thus, the number of steps required is at most the sum of the sizes of
@code{set1} and @code{set2}, rather than the product of the sizes as with the
unordered representation.  This is @math{{\Theta(n)}} growth rather than
@math{{\Theta(n^2)}}---a considerable speedup, even for sets of moderate size.

@quotation
