above still costs the same as @code{treeToList1} once translated literally.
Rewrite @code{treeToList2} using a single mutable @code{ArrayDeque} threaded
through the whole recursion, with @code{addFirst} standing in for
@code{cons}, and re-answer the question for that version.)

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.64}Exercise 2.64:} The following function
@code{listToTree} converts an ordered list to a balanced binary tree.  The
helper function @code{partialTree} takes as arguments a list of at least
@math{n} elements and the count @math{n}, and constructs a balanced tree
containing the first @math{n} elements of the list.  The result returned by
@code{partialTree} is a @code{Pair} whose first component is the constructed
tree and whose second component is the list of elements not included in the
tree.

@example kotlin
fun listToTree(elements: List<Long>): SetTree =
    partialTree(elements, elements.size).first

fun partialTree(
    elts: List<Long>,
    n: Int,
): Pair<SetTree, List<Long>> @{
    if (n == 0) return SetTree.Empty to elts
    val leftSize = (n - 1) / 2
    val (leftTree,
        nonLeftElts) = partialTree(elts,
        leftSize)
    val rightSize = n - (leftSize + 1)
    val thisEntry = nonLeftElts[0]
    val (rightTree, remainingElts) =
        partialTree(
            nonLeftElts.drop(1),
            rightSize,
        )
    return makeTree(
        thisEntry,
        leftTree,
        rightTree,
    ) to remainingElts
@}
@end example

